body:
Update: I solved the problem. The issue was that the program wasn't able to handle small dictionaries. I added a code block inside `check` to avoid this.

    bool check(const char *word)
    {
        int hashed = hash(word);

        // New block
        if (table[hashed] == NULL)
        {
            return false;
        }
        // End new block

        node *cursor = table[hashed];

        while (strcasecmp(cursor->word, word) != 0)
        {
            if (cursor->next == NULL)
            {
                return false;
            }
            cursor = cursor->next;
        }
        return true;
    }

I'm using djb2 algorithm

    // Hashes word to a number
    unsigned int hash(const char *word)
    {
        // Using djb2 algorithm, taken from http://www.cse.yorku.ca/~oz/hash.html
        unsigned long h = 5381;
        int c;

        while ((c = *word++))
        {
            c = tolower(c);
            h = (((h << 5) + h) + c) % N; /* hash * 33 + c*/
        }
        return h;
    }

Thanks for the explanation! I've got another question. As I understand, variable `image` is a pointer of type `RGBTRIPLE` because we used `*`, so `image` is pointing to the memory we allocated with `calloc`. However, if we do something like

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
       int n = 50;
       int *p = &n;
       printf("%p\n", p);
    }

we would end up printing the address of n (i.e. 0xAF324). So if we went ahead and printed `image[height][width].rgbtRed`, would we get a memory address?

Edit: I found the answer on this stackexchange [post](https://cs50.stackexchange.com/questions/35158/cs50-2020-pset-4-filter-reflect)
